#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Plot digitized symbols, origins and curve samples on Hsu (1989), Figure 4.

Requirements
------------
    python -m pip install matplotlib numpy pillow
    python -m pip install xlrd       # Only needed to read an Excel .xls workbook.

Examples
--------
    python plot_hsu1989.py
    python plot_hsu1989.py --no-show
    python plot_hsu1989.py --workbook hsu1989_digitized.xls --labels --no-show
    python plot_hsu1989.py --csv hsu1989_points.csv --theta 45,60,90
    python plot_hsu1989.py --per-series --no-show
    python plot_hsu1989.py --point-types origin,fitted_curve --no-show

The plotted positions are calculated from beta_deg and r_ratio. Stored x_px and
y_px are used ONLY to report the difference after a values-to-pixels round trip.
The grid calibration describes x = av + bv*y for each vertical grid line and
y = ah + bh*x for each horizontal grid line. Its coefficients are interpolated
in beta and R/R0 before those two equations are solved together.

The original JPEG is displayed without a geometric transformation. A high-DPI
output enlarges its existing pixels; it does not add detail to the source image.
Round-trip agreement checks data serialization and the calibration calculation.
It is not independent proof of symbol identification, completeness, or exact
recovery of the original experimental values. All previously flagged symbol
mappings were confirmed by the user. User-supplied coordinates are plotted
as diamonds, separately from original symbol observations. Origins are explicit constraints; curve
samples are readings of the printed fitted curves. Outside-figure requests
have blank ratios and are reported without plotting fabricated coordinates.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import base64
import csv
import hashlib
import io
import json
import math
import sys
import zipfile
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
from bisect import bisect_right
